The all-A-occupied ambient tensor description is legitimate in both proofs. The global P in a local word acts as identity on remote A factors after choosing their two-sign Hilbert factors. Every local Hamiltonian term and original jump has a local extension that preserves Gauss. Physical states need not factorize. Establishing ambient norm estimates and then restricting to the reducing physical space avoids treating a global Gauss projection as a local operator.

Both arguments use every term of

\[
H_4=-2\sum_{\{a,c\}:d(a,c)=2}S_{ac}^*S_{ac}
\]

on every P matter sector, with bounded two-star support. The root leaves the uniform overlap constants implicit; the PRE supplies conservative explicit budgets and verifies the cubic support bookkeeping separately. No numerical calibration of these constants is claimed by either.

The common load-bearing step is exact: all diagonal edge terms disjoint from a local support commute with the operator and with all remaining diagonal terms, hence cancel from its interaction-picture conjugation. Only one fixed edge neighborhood is added. This does not iterate under electric evolution and is uniform in electric values, K and time. The original resolved and coherent jumps remain bounded local operators. The full dissipator is retained; selecting an observed label does not remove the other channels.

The root uses a finite-cutoff Lieb-Robinson estimate. The PRE instead proves the needed chain estimate directly for bounded strongly continuous interaction-picture terms, using the infinite-dimensional-site framework of its separately pinned primary reference. The hypotheses agree: bounded local term norms, fixed range and overlap, disjoint-support commutation and contraction of the relevant propagators. Neither proof assumes norm continuity of the original rotor electric conjugation.

For the root's reference, [Barthel and Kliesch, arXiv:1111.4210v2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1111.4210v2), I checked the setting, Theorem 1, its complete proof and Appendices B–C. At finite cutoff, a commutator with a Hermitian local operator is a local Hamiltonian Liouvillian. Splitting a non-Hermitian B into its Hermitian real and imaginary parts therefore gives the needed estimate. The transformed Hamiltonian terms have bounded range and overlap; their superoperator norms are bounded by twice their Hamiltonian norms, uniformly in cutoff. This is the finite-dimensional specialization actually used. No theorem for an unbounded norm-continuous rotor generator is inferred from that source.

The exponential commutator estimate plus its trivial norm cap gives a local disturbance sum. Covering the fixed support by finitely many balls and summing cubic shells yields a bound proportional to \((1+\delta t)^3\). The root's implicit shell argument and the PRE's explicit integer-lattice shell count are compatible. Duhamel then puts the full open Heisenberg propagator outside the source; its unital complete positivity gives contraction. Both obtain

\[
\|T_\kappa^*(t)O-U(t)^*OU(t)\|
\le C_X\|O\|\kappa t(1+\delta t)^3,
\]

also capped by \(2\|O\|\). K does not enter the propagation constant. The factor \(\delta\) is a conservative operator-norm propagation scale, not the prepared packet's measured group velocity. This is shared independently reconstructed content, not a PRE-only contribution.

## 3. Explicit scrutiny of cutoff removal

Let \(\Pi_L\) be the finite-volume product of link projections onto \([-L,L]\). On the cutoff tensor space, compress each local Hamiltonian term and jump individually. Compression preserves its norm bound, local support and self-adjointness when appropriate. It also preserves Gauss because \(\Pi_L\) commutes with each constraint. These are box-compressed rotor operators, not finite-spin weighted shifts; no identity for the latter is being used.

For the convergence proof only, define bounded full-space extensions

\[
V_{Y,L}=\Pi_L V_Y\Pi_L,\qquad
B_{j,L}=\Pi_LB_j\Pi_L,
\qquad h_L=KD+\sum_YV_{Y,L}.
\]

The full-space zero extensions contain global projection factors and should not themselves be described as local operators outside the box. This causes no gap: locality is used on the finite cutoff tensor space first, and the full-space extensions are used only for fixed-graph convergence. Since \([D,\Pi_L]=0\), the box reduces \(h_L\), each jump and its adjoint, and the extended GKLS dynamics. Its restriction is precisely the finite-box calculation.

On each fixed graph, \(V_{Y,L}\to V_Y\), \(B_{j,L}\to B_j\) and their adjoints converge strongly, with uniform bounds. In the common KD interaction picture this convergence is uniform on the compact orbit of each fixed vector: strong convergence of uniformly bounded operators is uniform on compact subsets. Finite graph sums remain uniformly bounded. The vector Dyson series therefore converges termwise and is dominated by a factorial majorant. It gives strong convergence of the unitaries, uniformly on each compact time interval.

For trace-class evolution, a finite-rank input reduces each term \(B_{j,L}\rho B_{j,L}^*\), loss product and commutator to strong convergence on finitely many vectors. Uniform boundedness extends this to arbitrary trace class. The bounded interaction-picture series supplies strong trace-class convergence of the complete semigroups. Initial electric moment or generator-domain membership is unnecessary.

The observable compression \(O_L=\Pi_LO\Pi_L\) also converges strongly with its adjoint. Its full-space extension is zero outside the box. The finite-box norm inequality for O therefore extends to that block-diagonal operator with the same constant; no locality assertion for the global zero extension is needed. Pairing with arbitrary trace class and passing to the limit gives the rotor inequality. A useful explicit decomposition is

\[
\left|\operatorname{Tr}O_L\,T_{\kappa,L}(t)\rho
-\operatorname{Tr}O\,T_\kappa(t)\rho\right|
\le \|O\|\|T_{\kappa,L}(t)\rho-T_\kappa(t)\rho\|_1
+\left|\operatorname{Tr}(O_L-O)T_\kappa(t)\rho\right|\to0.
\]

The same reasoning applies to the Hamiltonian term. Thus no norm convergence of \(\Pi_L\), of the rotor shifts, or of the generators is required. The limiting argument is performed separately for each graph; the bound survives with uniform constants. It does not by itself construct an infinite-volume process or prove a uniform-volume microscopic approximation. The candidate preserves these distinctions.

## 4. Full factorial intensity and qualitative count limit

Root Eqs. (4)–(5) agree with PRE Eqs. (20)–(21):

\[
F_{jl}(s,u)=\kappa^2\operatorname{Tr}\left[
B_l^*B_l\,T_\kappa(u)(B_j\rho(s)B_j^*)\right],
\qquad
F_{jl}(s,0+)=\kappa^2\operatorname{Tr}[B_j^*B_l^*B_lB_j\rho(s)].
\]

Summing arbitrary intervening trajectories gives the trace-preserving full semigroup, and all earlier histories are already in \(\rho(s)=T_\kappa(s)\rho(0)\). The formula uses the full matter-dependent electric Hamiltonian and all H4 sectors. The effect is local on the full space. The old \(23I+W_p+W_p^*\) identity is used solely after restricting the Hamiltonian-reference evolution to the initial matter sector. The root's application chooses resolved labels, so it does not reintroduce the previously identified unproved shortcut that multiplies a first coherent output by two at positive lag. The formula itself also works for coherent labels if their actual B operators are retained.

There is no global \(e^{-30\kappa Vs}\) survival factor. These are unrestricted factorial counts, not globally first/next events. The boundary is a limiting density, with no positive mass for exact simultaneity. The PRE's terminal-filled-sector counterexample reinforces why the scalar-plus-Wilson expression cannot be used as the full effect after arbitrary births.

For a finite observation window \(I\subset[0,T]\), root Eq. (6) follows directly. If \(C_{jl}(I,b)\) counts all ordered label pairs with first time in I and positive lag at most b, then

\[
\frac{\mathbb EC_{jl}(I,b)}{\kappa^2b}
=\int_I\frac1b\int_0^b
\frac{F_{jl}(s,u)}{\kappa^2}\,du\,ds.
\]

For fixed parameters, strong continuity gives the pointwise boundary limit. Positivity and trace preservation give the integrable domination
\(F_{jl}/\kappa^2\le\|B_j\|^2\|B_l\|^2\). Dominated convergence yields the displayed integral of the boundary value. Equivalently, for a fixed finite box and normal input the integrand is jointly continuous on compact time sets. This is a new POST check of the root's precise window-and-lag count formulation; it is not retroactively claimed as the exact statement of the sealed PRE's disjoint-bin discussion.

For any sequence of fixed rows \((g_n,\kappa_n)\) with \(g_n\to0\), this convergence permits choosing \(b_n>0\) separately so that the normalized count-averaging error is at most, for example, \(g_n^4\). It gives existence with no uniform rate or lower bound on usable bin size. If a nonzero integrated signal is intended, the window must have \(\int_I|\chi_p(s)|^2ds>0\); packet nodes and zero-length windows do not become signals through this argument. No first-pair Bernoulli variance can be assigned to this count, which can contain multiple pairs.

Root Eq. (7) agrees with the old prepared-state result and the PRE comparison. Retaining the Gaussian factor instead of absorbing its expansion in \(O(g^4)\) is harmless. With \(\delta=1/(4\tau_*g^2)\) and fixed \(T/\tau_*\), the normalized background error is \(O(\kappa\tau_*g^{-6})\). Hence \(\kappa_g\tau_*=o(g^8)\) is sufficient to make it \(o(g^2)\). Both derivations independently obtain this conservative family. Neither identifies it as necessary, physically selected or evidence that a fixed-rate signal vanishes. Preparation constants remain fixed-volume. Pointwise microscopic counting-density convergence, a quantitative nonshrinking lag window, count variance, economical repetition and real-photon identification remain unproved here.

